      <span class="eyebrow">Choosing campaign types</span>
      <h2>More campaign types isn't better</h2>
      <p>A small budget spread across Search, Shopping, Performance Max, Display and YouTube means none of them collect enough conversions for Smart Bidding to learn. Most accounts do better on three, run properly.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:14px">Which three depends on how people find you, and that comes out of the account analysis rather than a template.</p>
      <div style="margin-top:26px"><a href="#contact" class="btn">Get a free Google Ads audit <span class="arw">&rarr;</span></a></div>
    </div>
    <div class="panel rv">
      <dl>
        <dt class="first">If people search for what you sell</dt>
        <dd>Search first, with brand and non-brand separated so you can see what's genuinely new demand.</dd>
        <dt>If you sell physical products</dt>
        <dd>Shopping and Performance Max, with the product feed treated as part of the campaign work.</dd>
        <dt>If demand needs creating</dt>
        <dd>YouTube and Demand Gen, fed by your own customer lists rather than broad interests.</dd>
        <dt>If you're a local service business</dt>
        <dd>Local Services Ads and geo-tight Search, measured on calls and booked jobs.</dd>
        <dt>If you have existing traffic</dt>
        <dd>Remarketing, with converters excluded and membership durations matched to your buying cycle.</dd>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

