<section class="wrap pt0">
  <div class="sec-head rv">
    <span class="eyebrow">Case studies</span>
    <h2>What changed, and why</h2>
    <p>Filter by the campaign type that did the heavy lifting. Figures are from live accounts we manage or have managed. Client names are withheld because most of this work is covered by confidentiality.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="filters rv" data-filterbar>
    <em>Campaign type</em>
    <button class="fbtn on" data-f="all">All</button>
    <button class="fbtn" data-f="search">Search</button>
    <button class="fbtn" data-f="shopping">Shopping</button>
    <button class="fbtn" data-f="pmax">Performance Max</button>
    <button class="fbtn" data-f="local">Local</button>
    <button class="fbtn" data-f="remarketing">Remarketing</button>
  </div>

  <div class="cases stg">
    <article class="case" data-tags="search">
      <div class="case-top">
        <span class="case-tag">Logistics</span>
        <span class="case-ch">Search<br>Negative keywords</span>
      </div>
      <div class="case-body">
        <h3>Nine hundred keywords, most of them buying the wrong traffic</h3>
        <p>Broad match with almost no negatives meant the account paid for job applicants, competitor research and freight categories the company does not handle. We cut spend back to the terms that produced quotes and rebuilt the negative lists from twelve months of search query data.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="case-metrics">
        <div><b>$25</b><span>Cost per conversion</span></div>
        <div><b>+52%</b><span>Conversions</span></div>
        <div><b>&minus;50%</b><span>Cost per click</span></div>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="case" data-tags="shopping pmax">
      <div class="case-top">
        <span class="case-tag">Skincare</span>
        <span class="case-ch">Shopping<br>Performance Max</span>
      </div>
      <div class="case-body">
        <h3>The feed was doing the campaign's job, badly</h3>
        <p>Manufacturer titles, no product type taxonomy and a third of the catalogue disapproved on missing identifiers. Rewriting titles around how people actually search, then separating Shopping from Performance Max, made the account legible before a single bid changed.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="case-metrics">
        <div><b>5&times;</b><span>Return on ad spend</span></div>
        <div><b>&minus;70%</b><span>Cost per conversion</span></div>
        <div><b>$12</b><span>Cost per conversion now</span></div>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="case" data-tags="pmax remarketing">
      <div class="case-top">
        <span class="case-tag">Fall-alert wearables</span>
        <span class="case-ch">Performance Max<br>Remarketing</span>
      </div>
      <div class="case-body">
        <h3>Performance Max was buying customers the brand already had</h3>
        <p>With no brand exclusions, PMax absorbed existing demand and reported it as new business, while remarketing chased people who had bought that same week. Excluding brand terms and capping frequency showed what the account was genuinely adding.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="case-metrics">
        <div><b>1.2&times; to 4&times;</b><span>Return on ad spend</span></div>
        <div><b>+80%</b><span>Conversions</span></div>
        <div><b>&minus;50%</b><span>Cost per conversion</span></div>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="case" data-tags="search remarketing">
      <div class="case-top">
        <span class="case-tag">Law firm</span>
        <span class="case-ch">Search<br>Remarketing</span>
      </div>
      <div class="case-body">
        <h3>Twenty hooks a month, three of them worth funding</h3>
        <p>Video ran on a single hero asset with no variation, so cost per view climbed as frequency rose. We moved to a monthly batch of short hooks, cut anything below the view rate threshold at day seven, and fed the survivors into Search remarketing.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="case-metrics">
        <div><b>$65</b><span>Cost per enquiry</span></div>
        <div><b>+25%</b><span>Conversions</span></div>
        <div><b>&minus;30%</b><span>Cost per click</span></div>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="case" data-tags="local search">
      <div class="case-top">
        <span class="case-tag">HVAC</span>
        <span class="case-ch">Local Services<br>Call tracking</span>
      </div>
      <div class="case-body">
        <h3>Half the recorded conversions were not real</h3>
        <p>Every call over fifteen seconds counted as a lead, including suppliers, recruiters and wrong numbers. Once calls were qualified against the booking system the true cost per job turned out to be far higher than reported, and bidding had been optimising towards noise.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="case-metrics">
        <div><b>&minus;33%</b><span>Cost per booked job</span></div>
        <div><b>+40%</b><span>Booked jobs</span></div>
        <div><b>&minus;40%</b><span>Cost per click</span></div>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="case" data-tags="search remarketing">
      <div class="case-top">
        <span class="case-tag">Apartment rentals</span>
        <span class="case-ch">Search<br>RLSA</span>
      </div>
      <div class="case-body">
        <h3>Remarketing was paying to reach current residents</h3>
        <p>Audience lists had no exclusion for people who had already signed, so budget reached tenants and recent applicants. Segmenting by tour booked and application started, then bidding only on the pre-tour stage, moved the money to people still deciding.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="case-metrics">
        <div><b>&minus;36%</b><span>Cost per tour</span></div>
        <div><b>+21%</b><span>Tours booked</span></div>
        <div><b>&minus;20%</b><span>Cost per click</span></div>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
  <p class="noresult hidden">No case studies for that campaign type yet. Try another filter.</p>
  </section>

<section class="wrap pt0" id="findings">
  <div class="sec-head rv">
    <span class="eyebrow">The $200 analysis</span>
    <h2>What we look for first in an <span class="hl">existing account</span></h2>
    <p>Six problems turn up often enough that we check for them before anything else. None are exotic, and every one of them costs money for as long as it goes unnoticed.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="channels stg">
    <div class="ch">
      <div class="ch-top"><h3>Conversion tracking that counts twice</h3><span class="unit">Tracking</span></div>
      <p>The same purchase firing from a hard-coded tag and a GA4 import, or a thank-you page counted on every refresh. Smart Bidding then optimises towards a number that was never real, and every report built on top of it inherits the error.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="ch">
      <div class="ch-top"><h3>Branded search eating the budget</h3><span class="unit">Wasted spend</span></div>
      <p>Brand terms convert cheaply and make an account look healthy, but most of those clicks were already yours for free. We separate brand from non-brand before judging anything, because averaging the two hides what the account is actually buying.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="ch">
      <div class="ch-top"><h3>Performance Max with no brand exclusions</h3><span class="unit">PMax</span></div>
      <p>Left unrestricted, PMax absorbs existing demand and reports it as new business. Without brand exclusions and a channel-level breakdown there is no way to tell what it genuinely added.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="ch">
      <div class="ch-top"><h3>Broad match with a thin negative list</h3><span class="unit">Search</span></div>
      <p>Broad match is only as good as the negatives behind it. We pull the search terms report back as far as it goes and usually find whole categories of traffic nobody chose to pay for.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="ch">
      <div class="ch-top"><h3>One conversion action for everything</h3><span class="unit">Bidding</span></div>
      <p>A newsletter signup and a signed contract weighted the same teaches the algorithm to chase whichever is easiest. Conversion values have to reflect what each action is actually worth to the business.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="ch">
      <div class="ch-top"><h3>A product feed doing the campaign's job</h3><span class="unit">Shopping</span></div>
      <p>Manufacturer titles, missing attributes and stale availability decide Shopping performance long before bidding does. Most feed problems are invisible in the Ads interface and only show up in Merchant Center.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

